<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Modern state-space models (SSMs) often utilize transition matrices which enable efficient computation but pose restrictions on the model’s expressivity, as measured in terms of the ability to emulate finite-state automata (FSA).
While unstructured transition matrices are optimal in terms of expressivity, they come at a prohibitively high compute and memory cost even for moderate state sizes.
We propose a structured sparse parametrization of transition matrices in SSMs that enables FSA state tracking with optimal state size and depth, while keeping the computational cost of the recurrence comparable to that of diagonal SSMs.
Our method, </span><em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PD-SSM</em><span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, parametrizes the transition matrix as the product of a column one-hot matrix (</span><math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and a complex-valued diagonal matrix (</span><math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
Consequently, the computational cost of parallel scans scales linearly with the state size.
Theoretically, the model is BIBO-stable and can emulate any </span><math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-state FSA with one layer of dimension </span><math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and a linear readout of size </span><math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, significantly improving on all current structured SSM guarantees.
Experimentally, the model significantly outperforms a wide collection of modern SSM variants on various FSA state tracking tasks.
On multiclass time-series classification, the performance is comparable to that of neural controlled differential equations, a paradigm explicitly built for time-series analysis.
Finally, we integrate PD-SSM into a hybrid Transformer-SSM architecture and demonstrate that the model can effectively track the states of a complex FSA in which transitions are encoded as a set of variable-length English sentences.
The code is available at </span><a href="https://github.com/IBM/expressive-sparse-state-space-model" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">https://github.com/IBM/expressive-sparse-state-space-model</a></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The Transformer </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. 2017</a><span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> marked a paradigm shift in machine learning, providing a unified yet versatile architecture with strong scalability.
However, experimental and theoretical results indicate that Transformers struggle in algorithmic state tracking tasks such as emulating finite-state automata (FSAs) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hahn 2020</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattamishra et al. 2020</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill and Sabharwal 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strobl et al. 2024</a><span id="S1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Moreover, the inherent quadratic cost of the attention mechanism hinders the training on very long sequences, and its linearly growing key/value cache can lead to memory issues during inference.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In contrast, </span><em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">state-space models</em><span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (SSMs) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2022b</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orvieto et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao 2023</a><span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> offer a scalable alternative to Transformers with complementary properties: they generalize better to longer sequences, the number of total operations scales linearly with the sequence length, and memory is constant during inference.
Modern large language models (LLMs) are increasingly adopting a hybrid architecture that integrates both Transformer and SSM layers </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Waleffe et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lenz et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025</a><span id="S1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
To achieve computational efficiency competitive with that of Transformers, modern SSMs often utilize diagonal transition matrices. Empirically, in time-invariant SSMs, this strategy has proven effective in a range of applications </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2022a</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orvieto et al. 2023</a><span id="S1.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, while time-varying diagonal SSMs can achieve language modeling performance comparable to that of the Transformer </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao 2023</a><span id="S1.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Despite their promising performance in language modeling, diagonal SSMs are restricted in the type of finite-state automaton (FSA) they can model </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. 2024</a><span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
This stands in stark contrast with the nonlinear RNN, which tends to quickly learn such algorithmic abstractions </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. 2023</a><span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
To enable FSA </span><em id="S1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">state tracking</em><span id="S1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, previous approaches propose using transition matrices of various structures such as fully unstructured (i.e., dense)  </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. 2025</a><span id="S1.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, or (semi-)structured matrices such as block-diagonal </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. 2024</a><span id="S1.p3.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p3.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, diagonal plus low-rank (DPLR) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p3.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al. 2021a</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. 2025</a><span id="S1.p3.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p3.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or products of DPLR matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siems et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S1.p3.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
While any </span><math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p3.1.27" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-state FSA can be represented by an unstructured SSM with a single layer, state dimensionality </span><math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p3.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and a readout size </span><math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p3.1.29" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, unstructured SSMs scale unfavorably and thus prohibit large-scale training. Current semi-structured approaches are more efficient, but do not allow for such compact encodings.
With this in mind, the goal of this work can be summarized as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We aim to enable single-layer time-variant SSMs with state size <math id="S1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> and readout size <math id="S1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> to represent any FSA with <math id="S1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> states while keeping the cost comparable to that of diagonal SSMs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To this end, we propose a novel structured sparse matrix parametrization of SSM transition matrices, denoted as </span><em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PD</em><span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> parametrization, defined as a product of a binary column-one-hot matrix (</span><math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and a complex-valued diagonal matrix (</span><math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
A PD parametrization of transition matrices is preserved under multiplication, meaning that the product of PD matrices is still a PD matrix. As such, a chained product of </span><math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> PD matrices can be computed using parallel scans </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blelloch 1990</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martin and Cundy 2018</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. 2023</a><span id="S1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in </span><math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(LN)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(LN)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> concurrent operations and </span><math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(LN)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(LN)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> memory, matching the complexity and concurrency of a chain of diagonal matrix products.
In terms of expressivity, the PD matrices encompass the commonly used diagonal transition matrices, but can also represent transition functions of arbitrary </span><math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-state FSAs. Our contributions are as follows:</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We propose PD-SSM, a time-variant SSM that can emulate arbitrary </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-state FSAs with a single layer, state dimension </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and a linear readout of size </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Theoretically, we prove that PD-SSM is stable and that it achieves universal FSA emulation with (almost) the least possible state size.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Empirically, we demonstrate that PD-SSM learns state tracking of complex FSAs by exhibiting state-of-the-art length generalization. We furthermore test our model on long-range multivariate time-series classification tasks and observe that it exhibits high performance, comparable to that of neural controlled differential equations.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We provide a novel benchmark in which transitions corresponding to FSAs are obscured by the semantics of natural language. Adopting a hybrid Transformer-SSM architecture, we empirically show that integrating PD-SSM solves the task and generalizes to longer sequences, whereas integration of diagonal SSMs fails to solve the task, even on in-domain sequences.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>State-Space Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">By </span><em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">state-space models</em><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (SSMs), we denote neural networks that utilize the recurrence equations</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=A(u_{t})x_{t-1}+B(u_{t})u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=A(u_{t})x_{t-1}+B(u_{t})u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=C(u_{t})x_{t}+D(u_{t})u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=C(u_{t})x_{t}+D(u_{t})u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\psi(y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\psi(y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the input to the system, </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t}\in\mathbb{C}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t}\in\mathbb{C}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> its (hidden) state, </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{t}\in\mathbb{C}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{t}\in\mathbb{C}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the complex-valued output, and </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle o_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle o_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the real-valued output. </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the state transition matrix, </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> map from embeddings to states and vice versa, and </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D(u_{t})\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a memoryless skip connection. Finally, </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi:\mathbb{C}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi:\mathbb{C}^{D}\to\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> extracts a real-valued embedding from a complex-valued embedding, often implemented as </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(y_{t})=Re\{y_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(y_{t})=Re\{y_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2022a</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orvieto et al. 2023</a><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
The time-variance of the system stems from the time dependence of </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t}),B(u_{t}),C(u_{t}),D(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t}),B(u_{t}),C(u_{t}),D(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In this work we focus on the </span><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrix, it being the crucial differentiator between time-varying and time-invariant SSMs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. 2024</a><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">If the system is time-invariant, i.e., </span><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})=A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, then its dynamics can be equivalently represented by a diagonal transition matrix ùp to an arbitrarily small perturbation of the entries of </span><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orvieto et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Axler 2024</a><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Indeed, writing </span><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A=W\Lambda W^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A=W\Lambda W^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> results, upon change of basis </span><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{x}_{t}:=W^{-1}x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{x}_{t}:=W^{-1}x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, in an equivalent state evolution </span><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{x}_{t}=\Lambda\tilde{x}_{t}+W^{-1}Bu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{x}_{t}=\Lambda\tilde{x}_{t}+W^{-1}Bu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> but with a diagonal state transition matrix </span><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In contrast, if </span><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a function of time, then the system admits a diagonal representation if and only if all </span><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are diagonalizable </span><em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">under the same change of basis</em><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. That is, there must exist </span><em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">a single</em><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W\in\mathbb{C}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> such that </span><em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for all</em><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})=W\Lambda_{t}W^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})=W\Lambda_{t}W^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This is a much more restrictive condition. It can in fact only be fulfilled if the matrix product commutes for all state transition matrices occurring over time, i.e., </span><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall i\neq j,A(u_{i})A(u_{j})=A(u_{j})A(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall i\neq j,A(u_{i})A(u_{j})=A(u_{j})A(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Axler 2024</a><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
This fact hints at the restrictiveness of diagonal transition matrices, which is expanded upon in an alternative framework in Section </span><a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Limitations of Time-Variant SSMs for State Tracking in FSAs ‣ 2 Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a></p>
</div>
