<tr id="S2.T1.3.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Structured Sparse</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PD-SSM (Ours)</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(LN)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(LN)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.20" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.21" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The structure of the transition matrix <math id="S2.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> in time-varying SSMs determines the class of FSA they can emulate. We consider two classes of FSAs, partitioned based on the solvability of their transformation group. <span id="S2.T1.21.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">✓</span> indicates that the structure enables the emulation of any <math id="S2.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math>-state FSA from the partition using one layer, state dimension <math id="S2.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> and a linear readout of dimension <math id="S2.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S2.T1.21.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">✓</span>* indicates that the matrix structure can emulate any automaton from the partition, but at potentially high dimension or depth, and for DPLR exponentially large linear layers. The indicated cost only considers the complexity of the recurrence. DPLR methods trade off FLOPs for increased parallelism and more optimal memory management. In the recurrent case the complexity is <math id="S2.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(LN^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(LN^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, but modern methods utilize parallel algorithms which scale quadratically in <math id="S2.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>]</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Modeling Finite-State Automata with SSMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In this work, we measure the expressivity of an SSM by considering the type of deterministic finite-state automaton (FSA) it can emulate.
A deterministic FSA is an abstract model of computation, typically defined as a 5-tuple </span><math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(Q,\Sigma,\delta,q_{\text{init}},F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">init</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(Q,\Sigma,\delta,q_{\text{init}},F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> where </span><math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a finite set of states, </span><math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a finite input alphabet, </span><math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta:Q\times\Sigma\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta:Q\times\Sigma\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the state transition function, </span><math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{\text{init}}\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">init</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{\text{init}}\in Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a fixed initial state, and </span><math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F\subseteq Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F\subseteq Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the set of accepting states.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Any (deterministic) FSA can be mapped to a time-variant SSM as follows.
Encode each </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q\in Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> using </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle enc:Q\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{|Q|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle enc:Q\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{|Q|}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> such that the encodings of different states are orthogonal. Note that orthogonality is a sufficient but not necessary condition for mapping an FSA to a selective SSM, as certain FSAs such as modular counters allow alternative, more compact, mappings to SSMs.
Given such an orthogonal encoding of states, we can map the state transition function </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta:Q\times\Sigma\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta:Q\times\Sigma\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to the state transition matrices </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> via </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(\sigma)={\scriptstyle\sum\nolimits}_{q\in Q}\ enc(\delta(q,\sigma))\cdot enc(q)^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(\sigma)={\scriptstyle\sum\nolimits}_{q\in Q}\ enc(\delta(q,\sigma))\cdot enc(q)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,
set </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C=I</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi=id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi=id</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Upon identification of </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle enc(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle enc(q)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with </span><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the SSM corresponds exactly to the FSA.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Limitations of Time-Variant SSMs for State Tracking in FSAs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To demonstrate the limitations of diagonal SSMs for state tracking in FSAs, it suffices to consider FSAs with group structure in their state transitions. The transformation group </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Straubing 1994</a><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> of such an automaton is then the algebraic group of invertible state-to-state mappings with function composition as a binary operation</span><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            A more complete background on algebra and results on SSM expressivity is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</span></span></span><span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
For conciseness, we equate such automata with their transformation group, thus saying, for instance, </span><em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">solvable automaton.</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Through a circuit complexity argument, </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> showed that non-solvable automata cannot be emulated using bounded-depth logarithmic precision diagonal time-variant SSMs, providing an upper bound on their expressivity. Through an explicit construction </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarrof et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> demonstrate that all solvable automata can be emulated using finite-precision complex-valued diagonal time-variant SSMs, although the depth of the SSM stack exhibits a non-trivial dependence on the complexity of the automaton.
Concretely, the SSM stack depth is proportional to the Krohn-Rhodes complexity of the transformation group </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Margolis et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
A more recent family of time-variant SSM models utilizes (products of) DPLR transition matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grazzi et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. [2025]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siems et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. While such matrices enable the emulation of any FSA, the necessary SSM depth is again proportional to the Krohn-Rhodes complexity of the automaton’s transformation group </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siems et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
In contrast, unstructured SSMs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. [2025]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> enable the emulation of any </span><math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-state FSA using a single layer, a state size of </span><math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and a readout size </span><math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. However, they scale poorly with the state size.
An overview of all of the statements is provided in Table </span><a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 State-Space Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Associative Scan for Fully Parallelizable Recurrence in SSMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We use parallel scans to concurrently compute the states of an SSM </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blelloch 1990</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martin and Cundy 2018</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. 2023</a><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Suppose a binary associative operator </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bullet</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, i.e., </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(a\bullet b)\bullet c=a\bullet(b\bullet c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(a\bullet b)\bullet c=a\bullet(b\bullet c)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,
and a sequence of </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> elements </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
The scan operation (sometimes referred to as </span><em id="S2.SS4.p1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">all-prefix-sum</em><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) then returns the sequence</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[a_{1},(a_{1}\bullet a_{2}),\ldots,(a_{1}\bullet a_{2}\bullet\cdots\bullet a_{L})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∙</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.055em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a_{1},(a_{1}\bullet a_{2}),\ldots,(a_{1}\bullet a_{2}\bullet\cdots\bullet a_{L})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Note that composition of the linear recurrence of a (time-variant) SSM </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t}=A_{t}x_{t-1}+b_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t}=A_{t}x_{t-1}+b_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> takes precisely the form of a parallel scan </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(A_{t+1},b_{t+1})\bullet(A_{t},b_{t})\mapsto(A_{t+1}A_{t},A_{t+1}b_{t}+b_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(A_{t+1},b_{t+1})\bullet(A_{t},b_{t})\mapsto(A_{t+1}A_{t},A_{t+1}b_{t}+b_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with prefix elements </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a_{t}=(A_{t},b_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a_{t}=(A_{t},b_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. According to </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blelloch</a></cite><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">1990</a></cite><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, Section 1.4), associative scans can be computed in </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(T_{\odot}\,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(T_{\odot}\,L)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> total operations (i.e., in work-efficient manner) with </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(\log L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(\log L)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> sequential steps, where </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\odot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\odot}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> represents the cost of matrix-matrix multiplication. For dense matrices </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,
</span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\odot}=O(N^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊙</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\odot}=O(N^{3})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and thus the associative scan quickly becomes prohibitively expensive
in deep learning settings. In contrast, if </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are diagonal matrices, </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\odot}=O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊙</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\odot}=O(N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In terms of memory, parallel scans with dense matrices use </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N^{2}L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N^{2}L)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> memory, whereas diagonal matrices lead to a </span><math id="S2.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(NL)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(NL)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> memory consumption.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>PD Parametrization for Efficient and Expressive Transition Matrices</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/pdssm_parametrization.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/202;" width="476" height="202"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The PD parametrization can be integrated into any selective SSM by adopting the shown architecture for generation of structured sparse state transition matrices <math id="S3.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})=P(u_{t})D(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})=P(u_{t})D(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prior research indicates that the expressiveness of time-varying SSMs can be significantly enhanced by relaxing the structural constraints of the transition matrix </span><math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to allow for non-diagonal forms</span></p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cirone et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. 2025</a><span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
As noted by </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, a relaxation to arbitrary matrices prohibitively increases the compute cost of parallel scans from </span><math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(LN)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(LN)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to </span><math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(LN^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(LN^{3})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
To circumvent these computational limitations, we instead propose to parameterize the transition matrices as the product </span><math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A(u_{t})=P(u_{t})D(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A(u_{t})=P(u_{t})D(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, where </span><math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is an input-dependent binary matrix in which each column has a single non-zero element, and </span><math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a complex-valued diagonal matrix.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Figure </span><a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 PD Parametrization for Efficient and Expressive Transition Matrices ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> presents our architecture for generating transition matrices with PD parametrization.
The diagonal matrices are generated by two nonlinear feed-forward neural networks, each with a single hidden layer employing the GeLU nonlinearity </span><math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{\text{gelu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">gelu</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{\text{gelu}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and a saturating nonlinearity in the form of the sigmoid </span><math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The two networks define a magnitude generator </span><math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|D(u_{t})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|D(u_{t})|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and a phase generator </span><math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi(D(u_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi(D(u_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle|D(u_{t})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle|D(u_{t})|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\sigma(W^{M}_{o}(\sigma_{\text{gelu}}(W_{i}^{M}u_{t}+b_{i}^{M})+b_{o}^{M}))\in(0,1)^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">gelu</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\sigma(W^{M}_{o}(\sigma_{\text{gelu}}(W_{i}^{M}u_{t}+b_{i}^{M})+b_{o}^{M}))\in(0,1)^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\phi(D(u_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\phi(D(u_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=2\pi\sigma(W^{P}_{o}(\sigma_{\text{gelu}}(W_{i}^{P}u_{t}+b_{i}^{P})+b_{o}^{P}))\in(0,2\pi)^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">gelu</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=2\pi\sigma(W^{P}_{o}(\sigma_{\text{gelu}}(W_{i}^{P}u_{t}+b_{i}^{P})+b_{o}^{P}))\in(0,2\pi)^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Concerning the column one-hot matrix </span><math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the input </span><math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> generates the weights </span><math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which are used to soft-select among a set of trainable transition matrices, written as the dictionary </span><math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\{M_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}\}_{i\in[K]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{M_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}\}_{i\in[K]}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with </span><math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> being a hyperparameter.
Sparsity of </span><math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is achieved by applying a column-wise hardmax after soft-selection of the state transition. More formally:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle s(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle s(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{softmax}(Su_{i})\in\Delta^{K-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{softmax}(Su_{i})\in\Delta^{K-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle M(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle M(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{K}s_{k}(u_{i})M_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\sum_{k=1}^{K}s_{k}(u_{i})M_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle P_{:,j}(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle P_{:,j}(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{hardmax}(M_{:,j}(u_{i}))\in\{0,1\}^{N}\text{ where }\text{hardmax(x)}_{i}:=\delta_{i,\arg\max_{j}x_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax</mtext><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup><msub><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000"> where </mtext><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax(x)</mtext></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{hardmax}(M_{:,j}(u_{i}))\in\{0,1\}^{N}\text{ where }\text{hardmax(x)}_{i}:=\delta_{i,\arg\max_{j}x_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The parametrization is motivated by </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which has shown that models utilizing normalized </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="S3.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> convex combination of dense transition matrices can achieve perfect length generalization on FSA emulation tasks and are parameter-efficient compared to alternative proposals such as e.g. </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasani et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. 2024</a><span id="S3.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S3.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The </span><math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> factors of our parametrization have complementary strengths for encoding FSAs into time-varying SSMs. The </span><math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrix enables emulating arbitrary FSAs, but the required dimension scales linearly with the number of states. For cyclic FSAs, which form a central building block of all solvable automata </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krohn and Rhodes 1965</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarrof et al. 2024</a><span id="S3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S3.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, complex diagonal matrices provide a more compact encoding compared to column one-hot matrices. Visual intuition is provided in Figure </span><a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 PD Parametrization for Efficient and Expressive Transition Matrices ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Additionally, as proven in the following section, the diagonal matrices provide a guarantee for the system’s BIBO stability as the magnitude of each entry lies in </span><math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/noncyclic.svg" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/294;" width="476" height="294"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A non-cyclic FSA and its two corresponding column one-hot transition matrices.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/cyclic.svg" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A cyclic FSA whose behavior can be emulated with a single complex number.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Any <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math>-state FSA can be encoded using sparse binary <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> transition matrices (a), but modular counters admit a more compact representation based on diagonal transition matrices (b).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Approximate Gradients</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Strictly speaking, </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{\partial P(u)}{\partial M(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{\partial P(u)}{\partial M(u)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a generalized function—it vanishes almost everywhere, except at isolated points where it exhibits Dirac delta-like singularities. To smooth these singularities over sets of non-zero measure, we approximate the hardmax with softmax during the backward pass.</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial P}{\partial M}=\frac{\partial\text{hardmax}(M)}{\partial M}\approx\frac{\partial\text{softmax}(M)}{\partial M}\\
" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≈</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial P}{\partial M}=\frac{\partial\text{hardmax}(M)}{\partial M}\approx\frac{\partial\text{softmax}(M)}{\partial M}\\
</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This is reminiscent of the </span><em id="S3.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">slope-annealed straight-through estimator</em><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paulus et al. 2021</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, yet we use stochasticity neither in the forward nor in the backward pass (ablations with stochastic categorical sampling in </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are reported in Appendix </span><a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).
Note that during the forward pass we do not relax the hardmax, since doing so would break the sparsity essential for an efficient parallel scan.
With tempered softmax in the low-temperature limit, the above expression becomes exact.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Algebraic Structure of the PD Parametrization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In this section, we formalize the set of transition matrices </span><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> that are decomposable into a binary one-hot column matrix </span><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and a complex diagonal matrix </span><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Note that our </span><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle PD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle PD</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrices exhibit such a column one-hot structure. Hence, all of the subsequent statements made for the abstract class </span><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> immediately also hold for our matrix parametrization. All proofs are in Appendix </span><a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proofs ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefn1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(One-hot Column Matrices)<span id="Thmdefn1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{M\times N}:=\{A\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times N}:\forall i\ \|A_{:,i}\|_{0}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.500em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.0835em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{M\times N}:=\{A\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times N}:\forall i\ \|A_{:,i}\|_{0}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|x\|_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|x\|_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-"norm" counting the number of non-zero entries in <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Under matrix multiplication as its binary operation, </span><math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> forms a monoid.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 1</span></span><span id="Thmproperty1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Algebraic Structure of One-hot Column Matrices)<span id="Thmproperty1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmproperty1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmproperty1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a monoid under matrix multiplication, i.e., it is closed under associative matrix multiplication and contains the identity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Closure under multiplication is essential for efficient chained matrix multiplication via parallel scans, because matrix multiplication in </span><math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can be implemented in </span><math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> instead of the usual </span><math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N^{3})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, as visualized in Figure </span><a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Algebraic Structure of the PD Parametrization ‣ 3 PD Parametrization for Efficient and Expressive Transition Matrices ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 2</span></span><span id="Thmproperty2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Computational Efficiency of Matrix Multiplication in <math id="Thmproperty2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmproperty2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C=AB\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C=AB\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in <math id="Thmproperty2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N)</annotation></semantics></math> arithmetic operations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/pd_matmul_color.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/106;" width="467" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><em id="S3.F3.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Left:</em><span id="S3.F3.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> The sparse matrices in <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> can be efficiently represented by separately storing the indices of the active elements and their values. <em id="S3.F3.13.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Center:</em> The indices of the matrix product are computed with a gather-scatter operation. <em id="S3.F3.13.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right:</em> The nonzero entries of the matrix product are computed using gather-scatter followed by element-wise multiplication.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Stability and Expressivity of the PD Parametrization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">If the PD parametrization is adopted for state transition matrices and if one ensures matrix entries inside the complex unit circle, then the state space model becomes provably bounded-input, bounded-output (BIBO) stable. Note that our parametrization of </span><math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|D(u_{t})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|D(u_{t})|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with a sigmoid-based MLP ensures all conditions for BIBO stability are met.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(System Stability under PD-Parametrization)<span id="Thmprop1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varepsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varepsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the state transition <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}=A_{k}x_{k-1}+b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}=A_{k}x_{k-1}+b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{k}\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}:\|A_{k}\|_{\infty}\leq 1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{k}\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}:\|A_{k}\|_{\infty}\leq 1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Let further <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|x_{0}\|_{2}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|x_{0}\|_{2}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|b_{k}\|_{2}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|b_{k}\|_{2}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x_{k}\|_{2}\leq\sqrt{N}B/\varepsilon\quad\forall k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x_{k}\|_{2}\leq\sqrt{N}B/\varepsilon\quad\forall k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As an immediate consequence of the mapping from FSAs to SSMs described in Section </span><a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Modeling Finite-State Automata with SSMs ‣ 2 Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, Proposition </span><a href="#Thmprop2" title="Proposition 2 (Expressivity of PD Parametrization). ‣ 3.3 Stability and Expressivity of the PD Parametrization ‣ 3 PD Parametrization for Efficient and Expressive Transition Matrices ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> holds:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmprop2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Expressivity of PD Parametrization)<span id="Thmprop2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Any FSA with <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> states can be exactly represented by a single-layer PD-SSM with a state size <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> and linear readout of size <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Not only can PD-SSMs represent any FSAs, they do so with the (almost) smallest state size possible, i.e., PD-SSMs are maximally expressive for regular languages.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmprop3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimality of PD Parametrization)<span id="Thmprop3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For any <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a finite-state automaton with <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> states that cannot be emulated by any single-layer SSM with state size less than <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N-1</annotation></semantics></math> under unique state encodings.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The assumption of unique state encodings, meaning that each state is represented by a single, unique vector, is a practical necessity for readout. Indeed, without unique state encodings (and with arbitrary precision), even a single-layer real SSM with state size </span><math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can represent any FSA, albeit in a format that requires exponentially large lookup tables to read out. Proposition </span><a href="#Thmmanualpropinner3" title="Proposition 4 (Arbitrary Precision and Readout). ‣ Appendix C Proofs ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> makes this statement exact.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmprop4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Arbitrary Precision and Readout)<span id="Thmprop4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Consider <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t+1}=x_{t}+b_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t+1}=x_{t}+b_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{t}=u_{t}\cdot k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{t}=u_{t}\cdot k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> input encodings and <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{t}=\sqrt{p_{t}}\in\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{t}=\sqrt{p_{t}}\in\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> time encodings, where <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t</annotation></semantics></math>-th prime. Then, any FSA can be encoded into this scalar-valued SSM under an appropriate lookup table as readout.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Runtime Measurements</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We start by benchmarking the effective runtime for performing a forward pass of a single SSM layer with variable state size on an NVIDIA A100-80GB GPU. The diagonal SSM is our model
</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;">
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">without the </span><math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrices, whereas SD-SSM uses unstructured transition matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. 2025</a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
As shown in Figure </span><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Runtime Measurements ‣ 4 Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, PD-SSM scales significantly better than SD-SSM. PD-SSM’s higher runtime compared to diagonal SSM stems primarily from the additional operations in the generation of P: the simultaneous soft selection of transition matrices </span><math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> across the sequence during parallel execution incurs additional compute and memory. Table </span><a href="#A5.T4" title="Table S4 ‣ 𝑃 Generation is the main bottleneck in PD-SSM ‣ E.1 Runtime Experiments ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S4</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in Appendix </span><a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> confirms these findings and suggests potential improvements in future work.</span></span>
</span><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:179.4pt;">
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.p1" class="ltx_para"><img src="2509.22284v1/runtimes.png" id="S4.SS1.p1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:235/111;" width="235" height="111" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</span>
<span id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<span class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Runtime comparison of single-layer SSMs with different transition matrix structures as a function of state size with sequence length <math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L=1024</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>FSA Emulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We first evaluate our model on a set of </span><em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">automaton state tracking</em><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> tasks, originally introduced in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The four tasks correspond to four finite-state automata of various complexities.
The benchmark is centered at evaluating the </span><em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">length generalization</em><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> of the models, which serves as a proxy of the model having learned the correct algorithmic abstraction, avoiding fixed-length </span><em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">shortcut</em><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> solutions </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Concretely, the models are trained for 100,000 steps on a dataset of randomly sampled sequences of symbolic inputs of length 3 to 40, and are evaluated on sequences of length 40–256. We extend the set of results from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which evaluates each model under only one varying hyperparameter choice, state dimensionality of 128 or 512, reporting the best obtained result. We instead fix the dimensionality to 128, finding that it is sufficient for high performance.
The other hyperparameters are fixed for each model.
All models are evaluated using two layers. Our Table </span><a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 FSA Emulation ‣ 4 Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> reports the mean and standard deviation of the best validation accuracy of five randomly initialized models. The set of evaluated models consists of recurrent and parallelizable models, where we </span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">bold</span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_uline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">underline</span><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> the best and second-best parallelizable model, respectively. Excluding the Transformer, the parallelizable models can be interpreted as SSMs with various transition matrix structures, namely diagonal (</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao [2023]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and our </span><math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> diagonal model defined by setting </span><math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to identity), diagonal plus low-rank (</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al. [2021a]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grazzi et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.29" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), product of diagonal plus low-rank </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.30" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siems et al. 2025</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.32" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.33" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, as well as alternative variants including block-diagonal, a Walsh-Hadamard matrix modulated by an input-dependent diagonal matrix, and a mixture of diagonal and dense matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.36" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. As we can read from the table, our model performs best, with a significant margin over the second-best method which utilizes dense matrices every </span><math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 16^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">16</mn><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 16^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.37" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> transition.</span></p>
</div>
