<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Background</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The goal of this section is to provide knowledge of abstract algebra and circuit complexity theory at a level sufficient to understand the motivation behind our paper. While the presentation is fully precise, we do omit certain details for clarity, and we often focus on providing an intuitive understanding by considering visual and textual explanations and examples for important concepts. This approach is highly inspired by </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carter [2009]</a></cite><span id="A1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. As the core of our work is the design and analysis of a novel neural network architecture, one does not need to have a deep understanding of these topics to understand our paper. As such, the entire Appendix A can be skipped without significantly affecting the understanding of our PD method. For a deeper treatment of the topics, one may consult the following resources: </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Straubing 1994</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carter 2009</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora and Barak 2009</a><span id="A1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We start by motivating the connection between abstract algebra and FSAs in </span><a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Algebra and Finite-State Automata ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a><span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We then explain the central algebraic concepts in </span><a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a><span id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, starting with the definition of a group and finishing with step-by-step reasoning about why a certain group is solvable. In </span><a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Circuit Complexity Theory ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a><span id="A1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we introduce the main relevant concepts and classes from circuit complexity theory. We finish in </span><a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 SSM Expressivity Results ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a><span id="A1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> by examining the most important results on the expressivity of SSMs that combine the two presented frameworks.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Algebra and Finite-State Automata</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Algebra is, broadly speaking, the study of </span><em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">sets</em><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">binary operations</em><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on elements of sets. The set elements can be of various nature. An intuitive example of an algebraic structure is the set of integers under addition, often denoted as </span><math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In more generality, one may consider sets of functions with the binary operation being function composition. This is in fact highly related to the study of finite-state automata (FSAs), where we consider </span><em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">sets of transition functions corresponding to the input elements</em><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and we study the effect of </span><em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">composing transition functions</em><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This described set is defined for any automaton </span><math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{A}=(Q,\Sigma,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{A}=(Q,\Sigma,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> as</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A)}=\{\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1,\dots,T}):T\in\mathbb{N},\forall i\in[T],\sigma_{i}\in\Sigma\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℕ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A)}=\{\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1,\dots,T}):T\in\mathbb{N},\forall i\in[T],\sigma_{i}\in\Sigma\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where </span><math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1,\dots,T}):Q\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1,\dots,T}):Q\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the transition function obtained by composing the transition functions of each input in the sequence, namely, </span><math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1,\dots,T})=\delta(\delta(\dots\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1}),\dots\sigma_{T-1}),\sigma_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1,\dots,T})=\delta(\delta(\dots\delta(\cdot,\sigma_{1}),\dots\sigma_{T-1}),\sigma_{T})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This set contains all of the transition functions that can be expressed by the given automaton.
The limitations of SSMs on FSA emulation stem from the discrepancy between </span><math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and the transition functions that SSMs with different transition matrix structures can express.
A motivating illustrative example is given in Figure </span><a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure S1 ‣ A.1 Algebra and Finite-State Automata ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, in which we consider the </span><math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> automaton and a time-variant SSM with diagonal transition matrices and </span><math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall t:b(u_{t})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall t:b(u_{t})=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Please note that this figure only serves to illuminate the connection between FSAs with algebraic structure and SSMs, with precise bounds being presented later.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.22284v1/Algebra_BG.png" id="A1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/93;" width="476" height="93" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F1.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S1</span>: </span><em id="A1.F1.20" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Left:</em><span id="A1.F1.21" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> A visualization of the <math id="A1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> automaton as we used it in our experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carter 2009</a>]</cite>. <em id="A1.F1.21.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Center:</em> The two transitions of <math id="A1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> equivalently represented in matrix form. <math id="A1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the set of all possible unique iterated products of the two matrices, of which there are a total of 60. <em id="A1.F1.21.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right:</em> A simplified diagonal SSM that omits the <math id="A1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B(u_{t})u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B(u_{t})u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> term. As the transition matrices are restricted to be diagonal, and since the two transition matrices represented in the center are not simultaneously diagonalizable, there seems to exist a discrepancy between <math id="A1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math> and the SSM.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The reasoning in the figure above is only valid for a single SSM layer with no </span><math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B(u_{t})u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B(u_{t})u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> term. In order to reason about several layers of the full model, recent work </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarrof et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> have utilized the frameworks of </span><em id="A1.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Abstract Algebra</em><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (A.2) and </span><em id="A1.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Circuit Complexity Theory</em><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (A.3).</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Algebraic Groups and their Properties</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We will now define algebraic objects that are of interest to us. We start with </span><em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">groups</em><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefn2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Algebraic Group)<span id="Thmdefn2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefn2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">An <em id="Thmdefn2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">algebraic group</em> is a set <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a binary operation <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> that is</span></p>
<ol id="A1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Associative:</span><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(a\cdot b)\cdot c=a\cdot(b\cdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(a\cdot b)\cdot c=a\cdot(b\cdot c)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for all </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a,b,c\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a,b,c\in G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Has a </span><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">neutral element</span><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e\in G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> such that </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\cdot e=e\cdot a=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\cdot e=e\cdot a=a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for all </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\in G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">And where every element has an </span><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">inverse</span><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">: for each </span><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\in G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, there exists </span><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a^{-1}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a^{-1}\in G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> such that </span><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle aa^{-1}=a^{-1}a=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle aa^{-1}=a^{-1}a=e</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:276.0pt;"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Algebraic groups can be graphically represented using </span><em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Cayley diagrams</em><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Starting from the neutral element, a Cayley diagram visualizes the structure of the group by applying the group operation in conjunction with a group element to the current element. This can be easily understood by considering the group of integers under addition modulo 4 (</span><math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) in the figure on the right. We draw the Cayley diagram by first drawing the neutral element, </span><math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Starting from </span><math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, all other elements are generated by iteratively applying </span><math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on the current element. For</span></p>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:51.7pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/Z_4.svg" id="A1.SS2.p2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:72/70;" width="72" height="70"></object>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">some groups, in order to generate all elements of the set, we need to consider more than one generating element. An example is the </span><math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> group. While Figure </span><a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure S1 ‣ A.1 Algebra and Finite-State Automata ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> refers to it as an </span><em id="A1.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">automaton</em><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the figure is equivalently the Cayley diagram of </span><math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with the two selected generating permutations.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A central property of groups is the </span><em id="A1.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">commutativity of the binary operation</em><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The binary operation of a group is commutative only if </span><math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall a,b\in G:a\cdot b=b\cdot a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall a,b\in G:a\cdot b=b\cdot a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/C2_C4_D4.svg" id="A1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/94;" width="215" height="94"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F2.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S2</span>: </span><span id="A1.F2.16" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cayley diagrams of the group <math id="A1.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and the dihedral group <math id="A1.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carter 2009</a>]</cite>. <math id="A1.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative group, while <math id="A1.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is not. Both are solvable, with solvability of <math id="A1.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> shown in Example <a href="#Thmexample3" title="Example 3. ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Solvability</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Another central property of groups is </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">solvability</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We will first present it informally and will then precisely define it. Loosely speaking, a group is </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">solvable</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> if it can be constructed from co-
</span><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.1pt;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.p1" class="ltx_para"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/C2_C4_decomp.svg" id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/83;" width="181" height="83"></object>
</span>
<span id="A1.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<span class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S3</span>: </span><span id="A1.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Composition of <math id="A1.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span>
</span></span><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span>
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:200.1pt;">
<span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">mmutative groups. The details of the construction are particularly easy to understand on the example of the </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> group, shown in Figure </span><a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure S3 ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> group is solvable, as it corresponds to the direct product of two commutative groups. While the </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> group shown previously is not commutative, it is solvable as it can be constructed from commutative groups in a similar manner as </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. However, </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> cannot be constructed using commutative groups </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carter [2009]</a></cite><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. To speak about solvability in more definite terms, we need to define several concepts. The re-</span></span>
</span><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
mainder of this subsection defines and explains solvability in concrete terms. It can be freely skipped without any effect on reading the rest of the appendix.
Firstly, we will define subgroups, which are in essence closed subsets of a group.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefn3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Subgroup)<span id="Thmdefn3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefn3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group . A subset <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H\subseteq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H\subseteq G</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="Thmdefn3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">subgroup</em> if <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math> is itself a group under the operation inherited from <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup if:</span></p>
<ol id="A1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e\in H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, where </span><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the identity element of </span><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For all </span><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a,b\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a,b\in H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the product </span><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\cdot b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\cdot b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is in </span><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For all </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\in H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the inverse </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is in </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Of central interest are </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">normal subgroups</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which is a particularly well-behaved category of subgroup. We will normal subgroups them to partition a group into a sequence of subgroups. The solvability of a group can be determined by examining the structure of a sequence of normal subgroups.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefn4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Normal Subgroup)<span id="Thmdefn4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A <em id="Thmdefn4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">normal subgroup</em> <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> of a group <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, written <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\triangleleft G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\triangleleft G</annotation></semantics></math>, is a subgroup invariant under conjugation by elements of <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., for each <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\in N</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle gng^{-1}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle gng^{-1}=n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g\in G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In particular, the sequence of group partitions we want to examine is the group’s </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">normal series</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefn5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Normal Series)<span id="Thmdefn5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefn5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A <em id="Thmdefn5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">normal series</em> of a group <math id="Thmdefn5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite sequence of subgroups</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e\}=G_{0}\triangleleft G_{1}\triangleleft\cdots\triangleleft G_{n}=G," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">⊲</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.111em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.111em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e\}=G_{0}\triangleleft G_{1}\triangleleft\cdots\triangleleft G_{n}=G,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">such that each subgroup <math id="Thmdefn5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal in the next one, i.e., <math id="Thmdefn5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{i}\triangleleft G_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{i}\triangleleft G_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefn5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i</annotation></semantics></math>, and the series terminates in the trivial group <math id="Thmdefn5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Apart from subgroups, we need to define </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">cosets</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, as they are a central concept when analyzing how groups decompose. A coset is a subset of the group formed by a subgroup, but in contrast to a subgroup, it is not necessarily closed under the group operation.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdefn6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Coset)<span id="Thmdefn6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefn6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group and <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math> a subgroup of <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>. For any element <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g\in G</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="Thmdefn6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">left coset</em> of <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="Thmdefn6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g</annotation></semantics></math> is the set</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gH:=\{gh:h\in H\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gH:=\{gh:h\in H\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Similarly, the <em id="Thmdefn6.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">right coset</em> is</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hg:=\{hg:h\in H\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hg:=\{hg:h\in H\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Cosets partition the group <math id="Thmdefn6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> into disjoint subsets of equal size.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Consider, for example, the partition of the integers into even and odd. As the following example shows, the even numbers are a normal subgroup, the odd numbers are a coset, and together they form all integers.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmexample1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The integers under addition form a group which we write as <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The even integers, written as <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\mathbb{Z^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\mathbb{Z^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, form a subgroup of <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the sum of two even numbers is again even and the neutral element, 0, is even.
In particular, this is a normal subgroup, an easily verifiable immediate consequence of the commutativity of addition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The odd integers are a coset of <math id="Thmexample1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> constructed from <math id="Thmexample1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\mathbb{Z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\mathbb{Z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding 1 to each even number. That is, the odd integers form the set <math id="Thmexample1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+2\mathbb{Z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+2\mathbb{Z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is not a subgroup, as it does not contain the neutral element.
The sets are disjoint, are of equal size by a 1-to-1 mapping of the elements, and together they form <math id="Thmexample1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It is precisely the decomposition of a group by use of a normal subgroup that we are interested in. This procedure creates a new group structure called a </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">factor group</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which describes the relation between a group and its normal subgroup.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7</span></span><span id="Thmdefn7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Factor Group)<span id="Thmdefn7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmdefn7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> be a normal subgroup of a group <math id="Thmdefn7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="Thmdefn7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">factor group</em> (or quotient group) <math id="Thmdefn7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G/N</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of left cosets of <math id="Thmdefn7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmdefn7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, concretely <math id="Thmdefn7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(G/N=\{gN:g\in G\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="true" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝐶𝐿𝑂𝑆𝐸</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(G/N=\{gN:g\in G\}</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with a natural group operation.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let us again consider the even and odd integers. We have seen that this is the full set of cosets of </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. If we abstract away the individual numbers and only consider the concepts of </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">even</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">odd</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we can see that the two concepts form a group structure.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2</span></span><span id="Thmexample2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Consider the set <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{\text{Even},\text{Odd}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Even</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Odd</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{\text{Even},\text{Odd}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the binary operation defined as per Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure S4 ‣ Example 2. ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S4</span></a>. This figure is in fact the Cayley diagram of <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/even_odd.svg" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/106;" width="238" height="106"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S4</span>: </span><span id="A1.F4.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The <em id="A1.F4.12.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Even</em> and <em id="A1.F4.12.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Odd</em> cosets of <math id="A1.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> themselves form a group under addition, in particular the factor group <math id="A1.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The even numbers act as the neutral element, addition is associative, and the integers are closed under addition.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A solvable group is one in which we can create a sequence of such factor groups with each factor group being commutative. The sequence should also end with the trivial group containing only the neutral element.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8</span></span><span id="Thmdefn8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Solvable Group)<span id="Thmdefn8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefn8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A finite group <math id="Thmdefn8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Thmdefn8.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">solvable</em> if there exists a finite sequence of subgroups</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e\}=G_{0}\triangleleft G_{1}\triangleleft\cdots\triangleleft G_{n}=G" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">⊲</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.111em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.111em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e\}=G_{0}\triangleleft G_{1}\triangleleft\cdots\triangleleft G_{n}=G</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">such that each <math id="Thmdefn8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of <math id="Thmdefn8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and each factor group <math id="Thmdefn8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{i+1}/G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{i+1}/G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative.
Equivalently, <math id="Thmdefn8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> has a normal series whose successive quotients are commutative groups.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We will understand these concepts better if we use them, for example, to argue about why </span><math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a solvable group.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3</span></span><span id="Thmexample3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Consider the group <math id="Thmexample3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G=\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G=\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can understand the group by considering its Cayley diagram visualized in Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure S5 ‣ Example 3. ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S5</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/C2_C4.svg" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/124;" width="143" height="124"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S5</span>: </span><span id="A1.F5.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The <math id="A1.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> group Cayley diagram.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmexample3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We can identify the elements <math id="Thmexample3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G=\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G=\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> using two integers <math id="Thmexample3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the group operation as <math id="Thmexample3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[a,b]+[c,d]=[a+c\text{ mod }2,b+d\text{ mod }4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000"> mod </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000"> mod </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[a,b]+[c,d]=[a+c\text{ mod }2,b+d\text{ mod }4]</annotation></semantics></math>. If we choose the upper-most element to be the neutral element <math id="Thmexample3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[0,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[0,0]</annotation></semantics></math>, and generate the rest of the group using <math id="Thmexample3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexample3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+[1,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+[1,0]</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the Cayley diagram shown below.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/C2_C4_Numbered.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:191/157;" width="191" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S6</span>: </span><span id="A1.F6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The <math id="A1.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> group Cayley diagram, labeled by integer tuples.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmexample3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We can first notice that the outer ring in the diagram forms a subgroup. Adding any two elements on this ring produces an element on that same ring, and the neutral element <math id="Thmexample3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[0,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[0,0]</annotation></semantics></math> is part of it. This group is isomorphic to the integers under addition modulo 4, written as <math id="Thmexample3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The outer ring is in fact a normal subgroup of <math id="Thmexample3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. When the group operation is commutative, as it is in this case, conjugation is a trivial operation and each subgroup is normal.
Concretely, if we use commutativity in the definition of a normal subgroup (Definition <a href="#Thmdefn4" title="Definition 4 (Normal Subgroup). ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we see that <math id="Thmexample3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle gng^{-1}=gg^{-1}n=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle gng^{-1}=gg^{-1}n=n</annotation></semantics></math> trivially holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The outer ring forms a normal subgroup isomorphic to <math id="Thmexample3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we know that we can use a normal subgroup to construct a factor group as per Definition <a href="#Thmdefn7" title="Definition 7 (Factor Group). ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. We have seen this in Example <a href="#Thmexample2" title="Example 2. ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, when we created a factor group dividing all integers using the even ones.
In this case of partitioning <math id="Thmexample3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G=\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G=\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="Thmexample3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the same factor group as we did in Example 2.
To this end, we will generate the coset <math id="Thmexample3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[1,0]+[0,\mathbb{Z}_{4}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[1,0]+[0,\mathbb{Z}_{4}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains all elements of the inner ring in Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure S6 ‣ Example 3. ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S6</span></a>. It is not a subgroup, as it does not contain the neutral element.
We can easily verify that the two cosets of <math id="Thmexample3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> follow the behavior shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure S7 ‣ Example 3. ‣ Solvability ‣ A.2 Algebraic Groups and their Properties ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S7</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/rings.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/79;" width="310" height="79"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S7</span>: </span><span id="A1.F7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The factor group obtained by dividing <math id="A1.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> by the outer ring normal subgroup.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmexample3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Abstracting away the details, this is conceptually the same figure as the one we have seen previously when dividing <math id="Thmexample3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmexample3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The factor group is therefore <math id="Thmexample3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have now constructed the first part of the normal series, <math id="Thmexample3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we know that the factor group <math id="Thmexample3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\mathbb{Z}_{4}=\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\mathbb{Z}_{4}=\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative because its group operation is isomorphic to integer addition modulo 2.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmexample3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We can now perform a similar exercise to derive that <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\mathbb{Z}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\mathbb{Z}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative group.
However, we spare ourselves the effort by first noting that <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{e\}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{e\}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative. Firstly, the trivial group is a normal subgroup of any group. Secondly, <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\{e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}/\{e\}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative because <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative and for each group <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G/\{e\}=G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G/\{e\}=G</annotation></semantics></math>. Both of these properties can be verified using the tools we introduced above.
With this, we complete the chain of normal subgroups with commutative factor groups, showing the solvability of <math id="Thmexample3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e\}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{4}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e\}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{4}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The entire exercise could of course have been skipped by directly noting that <math id="Thmexample3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative. In this case, trivially stating <math id="Thmexample3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{e\}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⊲</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{e\}\triangleleft\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> suffices.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We are now equipped with a basic understanding of what a solvable group is. To better understand the implications of this concept for state-tracking with SSMs, we need to introduce a different, yet perhaps surprisingly related framework </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Straubing 1994</a><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, that of </span><em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Boolean circuit complexity</em><span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p10.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Circuit Complexity Theory</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The theory of circuit complexity aims to provide an abstract understanding of highly parallel algorithms. One might imagine that there exist problems for which it is difficult, if not impossible, to design one efficient algorithm which operates on arbitrary-length inputs, yet in which all input lengths admit a bespoke, highly efficient and parallel algorithm</span><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            As per <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora and Barak 2009</a>]</cite>, introductory remark to Chapter 6, Karp and Lipton propose a variant of this idea in 1982.</span></span></span><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Such considerations are especially important in cryptography, as an efficient algorithm for the factorization of n-bit integers would be a significant development for any large </span><math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Boolean circuit complexity is the study of such parallel algorithms, or rather </span><em id="A1.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">circuit families</em><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Let us first define a Boolean circuit.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9</span></span><span id="Thmdefn9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Boolean Circuit)<span id="Thmdefn9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A <em id="Thmdefn9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Boolean circuit</em> is a directed acyclic graph where internal nodes are logic gates (typically AND, OR, and NOT), leaves are input variables or constants (<math id="Thmdefn9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmdefn9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1</annotation></semantics></math>), and there is a single designated output node. The circuit computes a Boolean function by propagating values from the inputs through the gates to the output.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A Boolean circuit family is simply the collection of problem-specific Boolean circuits, with one circuit being defined per input length.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10</span></span><span id="Thmdefn10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Boolean Circuit Family)<span id="Thmdefn10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A Boolean circuit family is a sequence of Boolean circuits <math id="Thmdefn10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{C_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{C_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each circuit <math id="Thmdefn10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmdefn10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math> input variables and produces a single output bit.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We will now present the two classes of circuits directly relevant to our work.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 11</span></span><span id="Thmdefn11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdefn11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> Circuit Complexity Classes)<span id="Thmdefn11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The class <math id="Thmdefn11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{TC}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝖳𝖢</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{TC}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of languages decidable by families of Boolean circuits of polynomial size <math id="Thmdefn11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(poly(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(poly(n))</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="Thmdefn11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(\log^{i}n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(\log^{i}n)</annotation></semantics></math>, with unbounded fan-in AND, OR, NOT, and majority (threshold) gates.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> consists of shallow (constant-depth) but arbitrary fan-in Boolean circuits of standard elements (AND, OR, NOT) augmented with a majority gate. Emulating solvable automata is proven to be within </span><math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarrof et al. 2024</a><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 12</span></span><span id="Thmdefn12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdefn12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> Circuit Complexity Class)<span id="Thmdefn12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The class <math id="Thmdefn12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{NC}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝖭𝖢</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{NC}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of languages decidable by families of Boolean circuits of polynomial size <math id="Thmdefn12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(poly(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(poly(n))</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="Thmdefn12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math>, with AND, OR, and NOT gates of fan-in 2.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> allows for logarithmic depth, but restricts the fan-in of the gates to 2. Emulating solvable automata is proven to be an </span><math id="A1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-complete problem </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barrington [1989]</a></cite><span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, meaning that all </span><math id="A1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> problems can be reduced to it.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Many relations between circuit complexity classes remain unknown. It is, for example, not known whether </span><math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}\neq NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}\neq NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
It is in fact not even known whether </span><math id="A1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is equal to </span><math id="A1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the class of problems decidable in polynomial time </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora and Barak 2009</a><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
As a consequence, certain restrictions have been placed on the generation of Boolean circuits in order to allow for a more realistic and fine-grained study of their properties. The central restriction is that of </span><em id="A1.SS3.p6.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">uniform generation</em><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, or </span><em id="A1.SS3.p6.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">uniformity</em><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 13</span></span><span id="Thmdefn13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform Boolean Circuits)<span id="Thmdefn13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A family of Boolean circuits <math id="Thmdefn13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{C_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{C_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Thmdefn13.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">uniform</em> if there exists a deterministic Turing machine that, given <math id="Thmdefn13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, outputs a description of <math id="Thmdefn13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in time bounded by some resource (e.g., logarithmic space or logarithmic time). Of particular interest to us is <em id="Thmdefn13.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">log-space uniformity</em>, in which the Turing machine operates using <math id="Thmdefn13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> space for input length <math id="Thmdefn13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/XOR_TC0.svg" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:390/129;" width="390" height="129"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A visualization of a uniformly generated circuit family, with the specific example being the XOR circuit for inputs of length 2. On the left we see a Turing machine whose input is <math id="A1.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 11</annotation></semantics></math>, signifying that the circuit should be generated for inputs of length 2. Either the size of the work tape or the number of computational steps is upper bounded as a function of the input length. The output tape contains the description of the circuit visualized on the right. This is in fact a <em id="A1.F8.10.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log-space uniform</em> circuit family, symbolized by the short work tape. The XOR circuit is highly related to the parity automaton, as it outputs <math id="A1.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the number of ones in the input is odd.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>SSM Expressivity Results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We finally arrive at the results that this section was building up to, bounds on the expressivity of SSMs within the framework of algebraic groups and Boolean circuits.
We do not prove any new theorems here nor restate the proofs, opting instead to refer to work which proves the claims.
Let us start by restating a classic result in the terminology that we introduced in our paper:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barrington [1989]</a></cite>, Theorem 5)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The word problem for any nonsolvable group is complete for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle AC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle AC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> reductions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The word problem of a group is defined as follows </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 14</span></span><span id="Thmdefn14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Group Word Problem)<span id="Thmdefn14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmdefn14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(M,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(M,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite group. The word problem of <math id="Thmdefn14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as evaluating the product of arbitrary sequences of elements of <math id="Thmdefn14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, given a sequence <math id="Thmdefn14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{0}m_{1}\dotsm_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{0}m_{1}\dotsm_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, solving the word problem involves returning <math id="Thmdefn14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefn14.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle m_{0}\cdot m_{1}\cdots m_{k}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{0}\cdot m_{1}\cdots m_{k}=m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The separation of word problems over solvable and non-solvable groups rests on the widely-held conjecture that </span><math id="A1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}\neq NC^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}\neq NC^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora and Barak [2009]</a></cite><span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, combined with the result that word problems of solvable groups belong to </span><math id="A1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mix Barrington et al. [1992]</a></cite>, Theorem 8)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The word problem for any solvable group is in <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A recent work analyses the expressivity of diagonal SSMs by demonstrating that the individual computations in the SSM can be emulated by </span><math id="A1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{logspace-uniform }TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">logspace-uniform </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{logspace-uniform }TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> circuits, under the assumption that the numerical representation capacity is logarithmic in the sequence length. Concretely, it is </span><math id="A1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a></cite>, Theorem 4.4)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Every fixed depth log-precision diagonal SSM can be emulated by a logspace-uniform <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> circuit.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This is the main upper bound we refer to in the paper, and is what we mean when we say that </span><em id="A1.SS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">non-solvable automata cannot be emulated by diagonal SSMs</em><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which is implied by combining the strict inclusion </span><math id="A1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{logspace-uniform }TC^{0}\subset TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">logspace-uniform </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">⊂</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{logspace-uniform }TC^{0}\subset TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with the conjectured </span><math id="A1.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TC^{0}\cap NC^{1}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∩</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TC^{0}\cap NC^{1}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and utilizing Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 ( [ ] , Theorem 4.4). ‣ A.4 SSM Expressivity Results ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This bound rests on an unproven conjecture, but it is supported by significant experimental evidence </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cirone et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grazzi et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarrof et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Using tools from <em id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rough path theory</em>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cirone et al. 2024</a>]</cite> prove the restrictiveness of diagonal time-variant SSMs as compared to dense ones without relying on unproven circuit complexity conjectures.</span></span></span><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Architectural Details</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Full Architecture</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This work mainly describes a new method for generating the SSM transition matrices. The method is itself embedded into a larger architecture, which mostly follows standard practice in neural network design as pioneered by the Transformer </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. 2017</a><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. That is, we embed the </span><math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle PD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle PD</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> parametrization into neural network layers which consist of nonlinear feed-forward networks, vector normalization, and residual connections, calling the entire model PD-SSM. The connection pattern of PD-SSM closely follows that of pre- or post-norm Transformers, and can be seen in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. 2020</a><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We show two sketches of the full PD-SSM model in Figure </span><a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure S9 ‣ Readout ‣ B.1 Full Architecture ‣ Appendix B Architectural Details ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S9</span></a><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Readout</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The readout from the state, </span><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, is different from standard practice, which typically implements it as </span><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=Re\{x_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=Re\{x_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orvieto et al. 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. 2022b</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. 2023</a><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Instead of taking the real part of </span><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we apply a transformation on the concatenation of the real and imaginary part of </span><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, denoted as </span><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Depending on the benchmark, the readout is either a GELU-activated nonlinear network:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read,O}\sigma_{Gelu}(W^{Read,I}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read,I})+b^{Read,O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read,O}\sigma_{Gelu}(W^{Read,I}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read,I})+b^{Read,O}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Or, if we refer to it as a linear readout</span><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              It is typical terminology to refer to trainable affine transformations as linear neural network layers.</span></span></span><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we concretely mean the affine transformation:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For the long-range arena tasks, we employ the nonlinear readout and a deeper architecture. For the state tracking tasks, we apply the linear readout and a single-layer architecture.
See Appendix </span><a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/pd_ssm_linear_model.svg" id="A2.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/765;" width="476" height="765"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F9.sf1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full PD-SSM model with a linear readout, as used in the state-tracking experiments.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/pd_ssm_mlp_model.svg" id="A2.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/769;" width="476" height="769"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F9.sf2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full PD-SSM model with a nonlinear readout, as used in the LRA and time-series experiments.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Full PD-SSM models we used in our state-tracking and LRA experiments. In the time-series classification experiments, we used a model equivalent to the one shown in subfigure <a href="#A2.F9.sf2" title="In Figure S9 ‣ Readout ‣ B.1 Full Architecture ‣ Appendix B Architectural Details ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(b)</span></a>, with the exception that <math id="A2.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{x}_{t}=Re\{x_{t}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{x}_{t}=Re\{x_{t}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Efficiently Computing the Parallel Scan Gradients</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The total derivative of a loss </span><math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with respect to </span><math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, written as </span><math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{dL}{dx_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{dL}{dx_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, satisfies the equation below:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{dL}{dx_{k}}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{k}}+\frac{dL}{\partial x_{k+1}}\frac{\partial x_{k+1}}{\partial x_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{dL}{dx_{k}}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{k}}+\frac{dL}{\partial x_{k+1}}\frac{\partial x_{k+1}}{\partial x_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In our notation, </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{dL}{dx_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{dL}{dx_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> explains the total effect of </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> via all of the intermediate computations, whereas </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denotes the direct effect of </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, ignoring the influence of </span><math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on the rest of the sequence.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The expression above is equivalent to:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{dL}{\partial x_{k}}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{k}}+\frac{dL}{dx_{k+1}}A_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{dL}{\partial x_{k}}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{k}}+\frac{dL}{dx_{k+1}}A_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We can see that computing the derivative of </span><math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with respect to the states </span><math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a </span><em id="A2.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">linear recurrence with sparse transition matrices <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></em><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Computing the gradient thus reduces to a reverse parallel scan with matrix multiplication complexity of </span><math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O(N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Within the framework of the parallel scan introduced in Section 2.4, gradient computation can then be parallelized using the following associative operation:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle(A_{i},\frac{dL}{dx_{i-1}})\bullet(A_{i+1},\frac{dL}{dx_{i}})=(A_{i}A_{i+1},\frac{dL}{dx_{i}}A_{i}+\frac{dL}{dx_{i-1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle(A_{i},\frac{dL}{dx_{i-1}})\bullet(A_{i+1},\frac{dL}{dx_{i}})=(A_{i}A_{i+1},\frac{dL}{dx_{i}}A_{i}+\frac{dL}{dx_{i-1}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proofs</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We first restate and prove the two properties of the space of column one-hot matrices, </span><math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 3</span></span><span id="Thmproperty3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Algebraic Structure of One-hot Column Matrices)<span id="Thmproperty3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmproperty3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmproperty3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a monoid under matrix multiplication, i.e., it is closed under associative matrix multiplication and contains the identity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It is easy to verify that <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the identity, as it is a column one-hot matrix. It is also trivial to verify that the product of two column one-hot matrices remains column one-hot. Given <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the columns of <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">select and scale</em> the columns of <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, the entry in row <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i</annotation></semantics></math>, column <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j</annotation></semantics></math> of the product matrix <math id="A3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C=AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C=AB</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^{N}A_{ik}B_{kj}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^{N}A_{ik}B_{kj}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Since for each fixed <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly one non-zero entry in column <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j</annotation></semantics></math>, say at row <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=r</annotation></semantics></math>, all other terms in the sum are zero. That is,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{kj}=0\quad\text{for all }k\neq r,\quad\text{and }B_{rj}\neq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{kj}=0\quad\text{for all }k\neq r,\quad\text{and }B_{rj}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">So the sum reduces to a single term:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{ij}=A_{ir}\cdot B_{rj}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{ij}=A_{ir}\cdot B_{rj}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Now, since matrix <math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A</annotation></semantics></math> also has exactly one non-zero entry in each column, the <math id="A3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="A3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A</annotation></semantics></math> has only one non-zero value, say at row <math id="A3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i=s</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ir}=0\quad\text{for all }i\neq s,\quad\text{and }A_{sr}\neq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ir}=0\quad\text{for all }i\neq s,\quad\text{and }A_{sr}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This implies that for each column <math id="A3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j</annotation></semantics></math> of the product <math id="A3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C</annotation></semantics></math>, there is exactly one row <math id="A3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{ij}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≠</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{ij}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and all other entries in that column are zero.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproperty4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_property">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproperty4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Property 4</span></span><span id="Thmproperty4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Computational Efficiency of Matrix Multiplication in <math id="Thmproperty4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmproperty4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproperty4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproperty4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproperty4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmproperty4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmproperty4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C=AB\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C=AB\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in <math id="Thmproperty4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N)</annotation></semantics></math> arithmetic operations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As per the previous proof, in order to compute <math id="A3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C=AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C=AB</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A,B\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we only need to compute one element in each column of <math id="A3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure 3 in the main text outlines the details of the computation.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The following proof is an application of a chain of inequalities that shows that under certain assumptions, which are fulfilled by the PD-parametrization, the SSM is BIBO-stable.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmanualpropinner1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_manualpropinner">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmanualpropinner1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmmanualpropinner1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(System Stability under PD-Parametrization)<span id="Thmmanualpropinner1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varepsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varepsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the state transition <math id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}=A_{k}x_{k-1}+b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}=A_{k}x_{k-1}+b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{k}\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}:\|A_{k}\|_{\infty}\leq 1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.278em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{k}\in\mathbb{H}^{N\times N}:\|A_{k}\|_{\infty}\leq 1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Let further <math id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|x_{0}\|_{2}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|x_{0}\|_{2}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|b_{k}\|_{2}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|b_{k}\|_{2}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmmanualpropinner1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it holds that</span></p>
<table id="A3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x_{k}\|_{2}\leq\sqrt{N}B/\varepsilon\quad\forall k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x_{k}\|_{2}\leq\sqrt{N}B/\varepsilon\quad\forall k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Expanding the recursion of the state transition results in</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}=(\prod_{j=k}^{1}A_{j})x_{0}+\sum_{t=1}^{k}(\prod_{j=k}^{t+1}A_{j})b_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></munderover><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}=(\prod_{j=k}^{1}A_{j})x_{0}+\sum_{t=1}^{k}(\prod_{j=k}^{t+1}A_{j})b_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where we define <math id="A3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\prod_{j=k}^{i}A_{j}=\mathbb{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝕀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\prod_{j=k}^{i}A_{j}=\mathbb{I}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="A3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝕀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the identity matrix.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We bound the norm of the state as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\|x_{k}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\|x_{k}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\|(\prod_{j=k}^{1}A_{j})x_{0}+\sum_{t=1}^{k}(\prod_{j=k}^{t+1}A_{j})b_{t}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00