<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Varying Mamba Depth on <math id="A5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mamba </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao 2023</a><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> uses non-negative real-valued transition matrices, which are in theory highly restrictive even for emulating solvable automata </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grazzi et al. 2024</a><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            Such models must converge to a steady state upon application of a constant input. This goes contrary to FSA emulation. For example, <em id="footnote7.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Parity</em> never converges upon repeated application of a 1.</span></span></span><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. However, we can observe that the model does in fact learn to emulate the non-solvable automaton on in-domain lengths, as shown in Figure </span><a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure S10 ‣ E.2 Varying Mamba Depth on 𝐴_5 ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S10</span></a><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It does, however, not exhibit any substantial length generalization.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/plot_mamba_A5.svg" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/124;" width="310" height="124"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F10.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S10</span>: </span><span id="A5.F10.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mamba with varying number of layers trained to emulate the non-solvable <math id="A5.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> automaton.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Additional State Tracking Results</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This set of experiments is based on the setup from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and measures the ability of a model to track the states of FSAs.
The one-hot input symbols are mapped to an embedding through a trainable dictionary.We use two FSAs from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, namely </span><em id="A5.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Parity</em><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><em id="A5.SS3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Arithmetic</em><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Furthermore, we define three FSAs with group structure; </span><math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{30}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℤ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">30</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{2}\times\mathbb{Z}_{30}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a commutative, solvable automaton, </span><math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{30}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">30</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{30}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a non-commutative solvable automaton, and </span><math id="A5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a non-solvable automaton. The models are trained to predict the final state of the automaton given a sequence of inputs, with no</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:138.0pt;"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">supervision on intermediate states. During training, random sequences of length up to 40 are sampled. During testing, the models are evaluated on sequences of length up to 500.
Both Mamba </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao 2023</a><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and our complex diagonal SSM defined by omitting the </span><math id="A5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrices struggle on non-solvable automata, confirming their theoretical expressivity summarized in Table </span><a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 State-Space Models ‣ 2 Background ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
In contrast, SD-SSMs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. 2025</a><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A5.SS3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and PD-SSMs excel at state tracking, with the latter being much more eficient. We provide fur-</span></p>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:189.8pt;">
          
<div id="A5.SS3.p2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task</span></th>
<th id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mamba</span></th>
<th id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Diag.</span></th>
<th id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">SD-SSM</span></th>
<th id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PD-SSM</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{2}\times C_{30}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">30</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{2}\times C_{30}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">24.1</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{30}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">D</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">30</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{30}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">18.9</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">61.0</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.3.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">A</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">10.6</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">8.3</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td>
<td id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A5.SS3.p2.p1.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">100</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
          
<figure id="A5.T5" class="ltx_table ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table S5: </span>Length generalization on FSA emulation tasks using single-layer SSMs. We report the average accuracy (%) over test sequences of length <math id="A5.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 500</annotation></semantics></math> for the best model from three random seeds of training.</figcaption>
</figure>
        
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ther ablations on the Arithmetic task in Appendix E.3, which demonstrate that (1) our deterministic approximation of </span><math id="A5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\partial P/\partial M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial P/\partial M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> outperforms a stochastic Gumbel-softmax straight-through estimator and that (2) the model achieves significantly better length generalization when using a linear readout function </span><math id="A5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>PD-SSM Ablations on <em id="A5.SS4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Arithmetic</em></h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We ablate several design choices on the </span><em id="A5.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Arithmetic</em><span id="A5.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> task from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A5.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Stochastic <math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math> Generation and Inference With Dense <math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math> Matrices:</h4>

<div id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The first ablation is centered on the generation of </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrices, in particular the following </span><em id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">deterministic</em><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> equations:</span></p>
<table id="A5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{hardmax}(M)\in\{0,1\}^{N}\text{ where }\text{hardmax(x)}_{i}:=\mathds{1}_{i=\arg\max_{j}x_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000"> where </mtext><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax(x)</mtext></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">𝟙</mn><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{hardmax}(M)\in\{0,1\}^{N}\text{ where }\text{hardmax(x)}_{i}:=\mathds{1}_{i=\arg\max_{j}x_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{\partial P}{\partial M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{\partial P}{\partial M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\frac{\partial\text{hardmax}(M)}{\partial M}\approx\frac{\partial\text{softmax}(M)}{\partial M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≈</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\frac{\partial\text{hardmax}(M)}{\partial M}\approx\frac{\partial\text{softmax}(M)}{\partial M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The dependence on </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> as well as the indices are omitted for notational simplicity.
Typically, straight-through gradient approximators, an example of which is our approximation of the hardmax via a softmax, are used in conjunction with categorical sampling schemes </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paulus et al. 2021</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al. 2017</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. 2013</a><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
In particular, a widely used </span><em id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">stochastic</em><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> scheme takes the following form, called </span><em id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Gumbel max</em><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">:</span></p>
<table id="A5.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{hardmax}(M+G)\in\{0,1\}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\text{hardmax}(M+G)\in\{0,1\}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{\partial P}{\partial M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\frac{\partial P}{\partial M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\frac{\partial\text{hardmax}(M+G)}{\partial M}\approx\frac{\partial\text{softmax}(M+G)}{\partial M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">hardmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">≈</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∂</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\frac{\partial\text{hardmax}(M+G)}{\partial M}\approx\frac{\partial\text{softmax}(M+G)}{\partial M}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\sim\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1),i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">Gumbel</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\sim\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1),i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Given the prominence of this scheme, we test its effectiveness on learning to emulate the </span><em id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Arithmetic</em><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> automaton.
Simultaneously, we test the effectiveness of using dense </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrices during inference.
While the </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrices must be sparse in order to ensure high efficiency during parallel training, at inference, the structure of the </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrix can be relaxed</span><span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
                
                
                
              The memory footprint of dense <math id="footnote8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" lspace="0.222em" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> matrices is <math id="footnote8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N^{2}L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N^{2}L)</annotation></semantics></math> during training but only <math id="footnote8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta(N^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">N</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta(N^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> during inference.</span></span></span><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> .
We test the effectiveness of employing the following </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> matrix generation during inference, </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle P_{:,j}=\text{softmax}(M_{:,j})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">softmax</mtext><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{:,j}=\text{softmax}(M_{:,j})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
This is optionally combined with stochasticity as shown above.
The best obtained results with all four combinations of deterministic/stochastic </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> generation and hardmax/softmax </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> activation are shown in Figure </span><a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure S11 ‣ Stochastic 𝑃 Generation and Inference With Dense 𝑃 Matrices: ‣ E.4 PD-SSM Ablations on Arithmetic ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S11</span></a><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/plot_gumbel_st_smax_amax.svg" id="A5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F11.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S11</span>: </span><math id="A5.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.F11.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> parametrization ablations on the <em id="A5.F11.8.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Arithmetic</em> FSA with 1 layer of PD-SSM with a linear readout. The dashed vertical line indicates the maximum training length.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We can see that introducing stochasticity and relaxing sparsity both have detrimental effects on accuracy.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Nonlinear Readout Experiments:</h4>

<div id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The second ablation is centered on the readout function </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. As previously stated, for our state-tracking experiments, the readout function is defined as</span></p>
<table id="A5.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with </span><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">⁣</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denoting vector concatenation.
Typically, however, neural networks interleave sequence processing layers with nonlinearly activated feed-forward networks </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. 2017</a><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, as opposed to the affine transformation that we applied.
We test the effect of a nonlinear readout, defined as</span></p>
<table id="A5.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read,O}\sigma_{Gelu}(W^{Read,I}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read,I})+b^{Read,O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">ψ</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" fence="false" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">O</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\psi(x_{t})=W^{Read,O}\sigma_{Gelu}(W^{Read,I}(Re\{x_{t}\}||Im\{x_{t}\})+b^{Read,I})+b^{Read,O}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We repeat all of the experiments that we report in Figure </span><a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure S11 ‣ Stochastic 𝑃 Generation and Inference With Dense 𝑃 Matrices: ‣ E.4 PD-SSM Ablations on Arithmetic ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S11</span></a><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, but now with the nonlinear readout. We see in Figure </span><a href="#A5.F12" title="Figure S12 ‣ Nonlinear Readout Experiments: ‣ E.4 PD-SSM Ablations on Arithmetic ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Structured Sparse Transition Matricesto Enable State Tracking in State-Space Models" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S12</span></a><span id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> that the results are significantly worse than with a linear readout. No method performs well even on in-domain lengths.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.22284v1/plot_gumbel_st_smax_amax_mlp.svg" id="A5.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F12.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S12</span>: </span><math id="A5.F12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A5.F12.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> parametrization ablations on the <em id="A5.F12.8.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Arithmetic</em> FSA with 1 layer of PD-SSM with a nonlinear readout. The dashed vertical line indicates the maximum training length.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">General Overview of SSMs</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LTI SSMs have been proposed as a more scalable alternative to Transformers that is also more performant on long sequences </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [2020]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [2021]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [2022b]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [2022a]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. [2022]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. [2023]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orvieto et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
A subsequent analysis showed promising language modeling performance with hybrid architectures based on gated SSMs and Attention </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. [2023]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Time-variant hybrid systems were then shown to perform significantly better on practical tasks with the Mamba SSM which utilizes diagonal transition matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu and Dao [2023]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Consequently, a line of work has demonstrated that hybrid models utilizing diagonal time-variant SSMs and attention exhibit better generalization to longer sequences, better general language modeling and reasoning performance </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al. 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lenz et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Waleffe et al. 2024</a><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Overview of Formal Results and SSM Matrix Structures</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Concurrently, formal analyses of time-varying SSMs have shown limitations of the diagonal transition matrix structure </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cirone et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, spurring a line of work investigating the utility of block-diagonal and unstructured matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terzić et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, as well as more expressive structured transition matrices, most notably diagonal plus low-rank </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grazzi et al. [2024]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. [2025]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siems et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, with the original inspiration stemming from at least as far back as fast-weight programmers </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al. [2021a]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al. [2021b]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidhuber [1992]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> whose computational structure contains products of generalized Householder matrices </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which enables the adoption of early algorithmic optimizations of such procedures </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bischof and Van Loan [1987]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Details of Formal Results</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As a lower bound on diagonal SSMs, </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarrof et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> show that a stack of complex diagonal SSM layers can emulate any solvable automaton, with the stack depth proportional to the Krohn-Rhodes (KR) complexity of the transformation semigroup </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krohn and Rhodes [1965]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Margolis et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
As an upper bound on diagonal SSMs, </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al. [2024]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> derives a bound on fixed-depth diagonal selective SSMs with logarithmic precision representation, placing them in the </span><math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{L-uniform }TC^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">L-uniform </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;" mathbackground="#FFFFFF" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{L-uniform }TC^{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> circuit complexity class.
This complexity class encompasses solvable automata.
DPLR matrices can emulate any automaton with depth proportional to KR-complexity and width linear in depth and arbitrary readout </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siems et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Experimental Expressivity Analyses</h4>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Experimental analysis of sequential neural networks trained on formal language tasks goes back to at least </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith and Zipser 1989</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hochreiter and Schmidhuber 1997</a><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The exact experimental setup we build on is a more recent one, with the set of tasks taken from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and the experimental setup borrowed from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walker et al. [2025]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. All of the non-solvable group word problems were generated using modified sample generation code from </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. [2023]</a></cite><span id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
